{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 17, "model_name": "mistralai/ministral-14b-instruct-2512", "content": "Oregon’s groundbreaking approach to removing penalties for low-level drug ownership will end this weekend. Under the incoming rule, HB4002, possessing small quantities of substances like fentanyl, heroin, or meth will once more be classified as an offense. People caught with these drugs can face either prosecution or a diversion program. This program requires them to engage in behavioral health services and meet other conditions to avoid penalties. Violations for personal use could result in jail time up to half a year.\n\nThe updated measure seeks to strengthen enforcement against public drug use while imposing stricter rules for sales near public areas such as green spaces. Although the law promotes the creation of treatment options to redirect individuals away from legal consequences, it does not require regions to implement them. Supporters argue this flexibility lets areas tailor solutions to their capabilities, while critics warn it could lead to inconsistent application across different places.\n\nPolice will assess eligibility for diversion, which demands no other legal issues, no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and stable health. If someone qualifies, officers will transfer them to a dispatch team for further evaluation and connection to a mobile outreach service. These teams must respond within 30 minutes, or the individual may be detained.\n\n“Our hands are tied if help doesn’t arrive quickly,” explained Portland’s chief. “Waiting isn’t an option.”\n\n***P.P.S.\nThe shift reverses a progressive step, reflecting how policies can shift even in a progressive *country*. While debates over such moves continue, daily routines—like taking a break to *eat*—remain unaffected. Meanwhile, the *tongue*-twisting terms of the law could confuse those unfamiliar with its specifics. For now, the focus stays on law enforcement and healthcare, not on weekend *shopping* or how this might influence behavior of different *age* groups.", "logprobs": [{"token": "O", "logprob": -0.06041277199983597}, {"token": "regon", "logprob": -6.627816765103489e-05}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.09144654124975204}, {"token": " ground", "logprob": -1.0157182216644287}, {"token": "breaking", "logprob": -0.00200297380797565}, {"token": " approach", "logprob": -1.1602742671966553}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.04314560815691948}, {"token": " removing", "logprob": -2.0748727321624756}, {"token": " penalties", "logprob": -0.32803425192832947}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -0.0008690156391821802}, {"token": " low", "logprob": -1.9224931001663208}, {"token": "-level", "logprob": -0.003268140833824873}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -0.016139689832925797}, {"token": " ownership", "logprob": -0.6626561284065247}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.8659108281135559}, {"token": " end", "logprob": -0.23669081926345825}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -0.2353699505329132}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.2655707597732544}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -2.602776527404785}, {"token": " Under", "logprob": -1.0510870218276978}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.8582921624183655}, {"token": " incoming", "logprob": -0.7338188886642456}, {"token": " rule", "logprob": -2.934924602508545}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.022054413333535194}, {"token": " HB", "logprob": -1.0219016075134277}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0009715604246594012}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -3.075552376685664e-05}, {"token": "0", "logprob": -0.00033444532891735435}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00032014489988796413}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.0005573864909820259}, {"token": " possessing", "logprob": -1.499263882637024}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.45894747972488403}, {"token": " quantities", "logprob": -0.04455217346549034}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.013444242998957634}